---
title: Elimination in square-root form
summary: A sum of squared linear residuals and linear terms is minimized, integrated or marginalized one block of variables at a time by orthogonal factorizations of its rows, without ever forming its normal equations; each elimination leaves a conditional and a smaller problem of the same form.
depends_on: []
---

<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import ModelGraph from '$lib/components/ModelGraph.svelte';
	import * as reflection from './statements/reflection.md';
	import * as reflectionBlock from './statements/reflection-block.md';
	import * as triangularRoot from './statements/triangular-root.md';
	import * as eliminateOne from './statements/eliminate-one.md';
	import * as marginal from './statements/marginal.md';
	import { starFactors, starTree } from './star.graph';
</script>

Gauss–Newton and Levenberg–Marquardt steps, Laplace approximations and Kalman filters all end in
the same piece of linear algebra: a function of a vector $\tangent \in \R^\dimension$ that is half
a sum of squared affine residuals, plus a linear term,

$$
	\phi(\tangent) = \tfrac12 \lVert A \tangent - b \rVert^2 + \eta\transpose \tangent , @tag(problem)
$$

which is to be minimized, integrated as $e^{-\phi}$, or reduced to a function of some of its
variables. This note shows how to do each without forming the matrix $A\transpose A$, and how to
do it one block of variables at a time when $A$ is sparse. It is a note about numbers and matrices
alone, and nothing in it depends on where the rows came from.

## Linear least squares

With $\eta = 0$, minimizing [](eq:problem) is the *linear least-squares problem*. Its gradient
$A\transpose (A \tangent - b)$ vanishes at the minimizer, which therefore solves the *normal
equations*

$$
	A\transpose A\, \tangent = A\transpose b . @tag(normal-equations)
$$

They are the obvious way to compute it, and the wrong one. Floating-point arithmetic rounds each
operation with a relative error of at most the *unit roundoff* $u$, about $1.1 \times 10^{-16}$ in
double precision, and a linear system amplifies such errors by its *condition number*
$\kappa(M) = \sigma_{\max}(M) / \sigma_{\min}(M)$, the ratio of its extreme singular values: a
solution computed stably is accurate to about $u\, \kappa$, so that a solve loses about
$\log_{10} \kappa$ of its sixteen digits. The singular values of $A\transpose A$ are the squares of
those of $A$, so $\kappa(A\transpose A) = \kappa(A)^2$, and forming the normal equations doubles
the digits lost. A Jacobian with $\kappa(A) = 10^6$, common when variables have different scales,
leaves ten digits to a method that works with $A$ and four to one that works with $A\transpose A$.

The loss can be total. With $\varepsilon = 10^{-8}$, take

$$
	A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ \varepsilon & 0 \\ 0 & \varepsilon \end{bmatrix} ,
	\qquad
	b = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ \varepsilon \\ \varepsilon \end{bmatrix} .
$$

The least-squares solution is $(1, 1)$, with zero residual, and $\kappa(A) \approx 1.4 \times 10^8$,
well within double precision. But
$A\transpose A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 + \varepsilon^2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + \varepsilon^2 \end{smallmatrix}\right]$,
and $1 + 10^{-16}$ rounds to $1$, so the matrix of the normal equations, as computed, is singular.
The information was lost when the matrix was formed, and no solver of [](eq:normal-equations) can
recover it. The factorization below returns $(1, 1)$ to full precision.

## Householder reflections

A square matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if $Q\transpose Q = I$. It preserves lengths, so it can be applied
to the residual without changing the problem, $\lVert A \tangent - b \rVert = \lVert Q\transpose(A
\tangent - b) \rVert$, and its condition number is one, so applying it amplifies no error. The
orthogonal matrices used here are reflections: $H_w$ below fixes every vector orthogonal to $w$ and
sends $w$ to $-w$, so it mirrors space in the hyperplane orthogonal to $w$. A tuple of vectors,
such as $(v, u)$, stands for the column vector that stacks them, $v$ above $u$.

<Statement {...reflection} />

<Proof>

$H_w$ is symmetric because $w w\transpose$ is, and

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		H_w\transpose H_w &= H_w^2 \\
		&= I - 4 \frac{w w\transpose}{\lVert w \rVert^2} + 4 \frac{w\, (w\transpose w)\, w\transpose}{\lVert w \rVert^4} \\
		&= I .
	\end{aligned}
$$

For the second part, $w$ differs from $x$ only in its first entry, by $-\alpha$, and
$\alpha^2 = \lVert x \rVert^2$, so $w\transpose x = \lVert x \rVert^2 - \alpha x_1$ and

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\lVert w \rVert^2
		&= \lVert x \rVert^2 - 2 \alpha x_1 + \alpha^2 \\
		&= 2 \bigl( \lVert x \rVert^2 - \alpha x_1 \bigr) \\
		&= 2\, w\transpose x .
	\end{aligned}
$$

The product $-\alpha x_1$ is $\lvert x_1 \rvert \lVert x \rVert$, which is not negative, so
$\lVert w \rVert^2 \ge 2 \lVert x \rVert^2$, which is positive. Then

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		H_w x &= x - 2 \frac{w\transpose x}{\lVert w \rVert^2}\, w \\
		&= x - w \\
		&= \begin{bmatrix} \alpha \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} .
	\end{aligned}
$$

</Proof>

Either sign of $\alpha$ would do, as long as $w \neq 0$; the one chosen makes
$w_1 = x_1 + \operatorname{sgn}(x_1) \lVert x \rVert$ a sum of two numbers of the same sign. The
other sign subtracts them, and when $x$ is already close to a multiple of the first coordinate
vector they nearly cancel, and $w$ is computed with a large relative error.

A reflection built from a vector whose leading entries are zero touches only the trailing entries
of what it is applied to.

<Statement {...reflectionBlock} />

<Proof>

The norms agree, $\lVert w \rVert = \lVert w' \rVert$, and the leading zeros of $w$ meet $v$, so

$$
	w\transpose \begin{bmatrix} v \\ u \end{bmatrix} = w'^\top u .
$$

Therefore

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		H_w \begin{bmatrix} v \\ u \end{bmatrix}
		&= \begin{bmatrix} v \\ u \end{bmatrix} - 2 \frac{w'^\top u}{\lVert w' \rVert^2} \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ w' \end{bmatrix} \\
		&= \begin{bmatrix} v \\ H_{w'} u \end{bmatrix} .
	\end{aligned}
$$

Read column by column, with $(v, u)$ each coordinate vector in turn, this is the block form.

</Proof>

## The triangular root

<Statement {...triangularRoot} />

<Proof>

The matrix $Q$ is built from $n$ reflections, one for each column of $A$, each of them made by
[%full](statement:reflection) to reduce one column, and each leaving the columns already reduced
alone by [%full](statement:reflection-block). Let $M_0 = [A \;\, b]$ and $M_j = H_j M_{j-1}$ for
$j = 1, \dots, n$, with $H_j$ chosen as follows. Suppose that each of the first $j - 1$ columns of
$M_{j-1}$ is zero below its diagonal entry, which holds for $j = 1$ since there are none. Let $x$
be the entries of column $j$ of $M_{j-1}$ in rows $j$ to $m$. Then $x \neq 0$: otherwise the first
$j$ columns of $M_{j-1}$ would all lie in the span of the first $j - 1$ coordinate vectors, and so
be linearly dependent, but they are the first $j$ columns of $H_{j-1} \cdots H_1 A$, which has rank
$n$ since each $H_i$ is invertible. Take $\alpha_j$ and $w'$ for $x$ from
[%full](statement:reflection), and let $H_j = H_w$ with $w = (0, w')$, its first $j - 1$ entries
zero. By [%full](statement:reflection-block), $H_j$ leaves rows $1$ to $j - 1$ of $M_{j-1}$
unchanged, and its first $j - 1$ columns too, since each is zero below row $j - 1$; and it maps
column $j$'s entries in rows $j$ to $m$ to $\alpha_j$ followed by zeros. So the first $j$ columns
of $M_j$ are zero below their diagonal entries.

After $n$ steps, $M_n = H_n \cdots H_1 [A \;\, b]$ has the form of the theorem, with $R$ upper
triangular with diagonal entries $\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_n$, each nonzero. Each $H_j$ is
symmetric and orthogonal, so $Q = H_1 \cdots H_n$ is orthogonal and
$Q\transpose = H_n \cdots H_1$. Then

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\lVert A \tangent - b \rVert^2
		&= \bigl\lVert Q\transpose (A \tangent - b) \bigr\rVert^2 \\
		&= \left\lVert \begin{bmatrix} R \tangent - d \\ -e \end{bmatrix} \right\rVert^2 \\
		&= \lVert R \tangent - d \rVert^2 + \lVert e \rVert^2 ,
	\end{aligned}
$$

and

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		A\transpose A &= A\transpose Q Q\transpose A \\
		&= \begin{bmatrix} R \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}\transpose \begin{bmatrix} R \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
		&= R\transpose R .
	\end{aligned}
$$

</Proof>

The minimizer of $\lVert A \tangent - b \rVert^2$ therefore solves the triangular system
$R \tangent = d$, and the minimum is $\lVert e \rVert^2$. $R$ is a square root of the normal
equations' matrix, $R\transpose R = A\transpose A$, computed without forming that matrix, and
$\kappa(R) = \kappa(A)$. A triangular system is solved by *back substitution*, from the last
unknown up: for $i = n, n - 1, \dots, 1$,

$$
	\tangent_i = \frac{1}{R_{ii}} \Bigl( d_i - \sum_{j > i} R_{ij}\, \tangent_j \Bigr) , @tag(back-substitution)
$$

the sum being empty for $i = n$, in about $n^2$ operations, and backward stably; a lower-triangular one, such as
$R\transpose y = c$, by *forward substitution*, from the first unknown down. A zero diagonal entry
means $A$ does not have rank $n$: some direction is not determined by the rows, and a diagonal
entry small against the others means a direction determined poorly. Either is reported, not
divided by.

For example, take

$$
	A = \begin{bmatrix} -2 & 0 \\ 1 & 5 \\ 2 & 5 \end{bmatrix} ,
	\qquad
	b = \begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 16 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix} .
$$

The first column is $x = (-2, 1, 2)$, with $\lVert x \rVert = 3$ and $x_1 < 0$, so $\alpha_1 = 3$
and $w = (-5, 1, 2)$, with $\lVert w \rVert^2 = 30$. $H_1$ maps each column $y$ of $[A \;\, b]$ to
$y - (w\transpose y / 15)\, w$, so the first column goes to $(3, 0, 0)$, as
[%full](statement:reflection) says, and the others follow with $w\transpose y = 15$ and
$w\transpose b = 45$:

$$
	\begin{gathered}
		H_1 \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} - \frac{15}{15} \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} , \\
		H_1 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 5 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 5 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix} - \frac{15}{15} \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 4 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} , \\
		H_1 \begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 16 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 16 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix} - \frac{45}{15} \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 8 \\ 13 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix} .
	\end{gathered}
$$

Together,

$$
	H_1 \begin{bmatrix} A & b \end{bmatrix}
	= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 5 & 8 \\ 0 & 4 & 13 \\ 0 & 3 & -9 \end{bmatrix} .
$$

The first row and the first column are now final. The second step works on what is left, rows 2
and 3 of the last two columns. There $x = (4, 3)$, with $\lVert x \rVert = 5$ and $x_1 > 0$, so
$\alpha_2 = -5$ and $w' = (9, 3)$, with $\lVert w' \rVert^2 = 90$, and $H_2 = H_w$ for
$w = (0, 9, 3)$. By [%full](statement:reflection-block), $H_2$ leaves the first row, $[3 \;\, 5 \;\, 8]$,
alone, and the first column, $(3, 0, 0)$, which is of the form $(v, 0)$. On the rest it acts as
$H_{w'}$, which maps $y$ to $y - (w'^\top y / 45)\, w'$:

$$
	\begin{gathered}
		H_{w'} \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} - \frac{45}{45} \begin{bmatrix} 9 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} , \\
		H_{w'} \begin{bmatrix} 13 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 13 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix} - \frac{90}{45} \begin{bmatrix} 9 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ -15 \end{bmatrix} .
	\end{gathered}
$$

Together,

$$
	H_2 H_1 \begin{bmatrix} A & b \end{bmatrix}
	= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 5 & 8 \\ 0 & -5 & -5 \\ 0 & 0 & -15 \end{bmatrix} .
$$

So $R = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 3 & 5 \\ 0 & -5 \end{smallmatrix}\right]$, $d = (8, -5)$ and
$e = -15$. Back substitution gives the minimizer,

$$
	\begin{gathered}
		\tangent_2 = \frac{-5}{-5} = 1 , \\
		\tangent_1 = \frac{8 - 5 \tangent_2}{3} = 1 ,
	\end{gathered}
$$

and the minimum of $\lVert A \tangent - b \rVert^2$ is $e^2 = 225$. Indeed, and with the root as
the theorem says,

$$
	\begin{gathered}
		b - A \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ 10 \\ -10 \end{bmatrix} , \\
		R\transpose R = \begin{bmatrix} 9 & 15 \\ 15 & 50 \end{bmatrix} = A\transpose A .
	\end{gathered}
$$

The second diagonal entry of $R$ is negative, which the sign rule of [%full](statement:reflection)
allows and the theorem does not mind. Changing the sign of a row of $[R \;\, d]$, which is
multiplying $Q$ by a diagonal matrix of signs, changes nothing in the theorem, so a positive
diagonal can be had when it is wanted.

Neither $Q$ nor any $H_j$ is formed. A reflection is applied to a vector $y$ of $p$ entries as
$y - \beta\, (w\transpose y)\, w$, with $\beta = 2 / \lVert w \rVert^2$, in about $4p$ operations,
and only the vectors $w$ are kept, if $Q$ is needed again at all. Step $j$ applies one to the last
$n - j$ columns of $A$, in rows $j$ to $m$, so the reduction takes about
$\sum_j 4 (m - j)(n - j) \approx 2 m n^2 - \tfrac23 n^3$ operations. Forming $A\transpose A$ and
factoring it takes about $m n^2 + \tfrac13 n^3$, so when $m$ is much larger than $n$ the
reflections cost twice as much, which is the price of the digits kept. Applying them to $b$
alongside $A$ gives $d$ and $e$ in the same pass.

## Linear terms

A linear term $\eta\transpose \tangent$ in [](eq:problem) is not a squared residual, but it can be
carried along. With $R$ and $d$ from [%full](statement:triangular-root) and $y = R^{-\top} \eta$,
the solution of the forward substitution $R\transpose y = \eta$, so that
$\eta\transpose \tangent = y\transpose R \tangent$, completing the square gives

$$
	\phi(\tangent) = \tfrac12 \bigl\lVert R \tangent - (d - y) \bigr\rVert^2
	+ \tfrac12 \lVert e \rVert^2 + y\transpose d - \tfrac12 \lVert y \rVert^2 . @tag(complete-square)
$$

So $\phi$ is minimized at $\tangent^* = R^{-1}(d - y)$, which solves
$A\transpose A\, \tangent^* = A\transpose b - \eta$, and the decrease from $\tangent = 0$ is

$$
	\phi(0) - \phi(\tangent^*) = \tfrac12 \lVert d - y \rVert^2 = \tfrac12 \lVert R \tangent^* \rVert^2 , @tag(decrease)
$$

since $\phi(0) = \tfrac12 \lVert d \rVert^2 + \tfrac12 \lVert e \rVert^2$. Two things change with
the linear term.

- **It needs every direction determined.** When $A$ has rank $n$, $\phi$ is bounded below whatever
  $\eta$ is. When it does not, $\phi$ is bounded below only if $\eta$ lies in the row space of $A$,
  and the minimizer is then not unique. The rank condition is checked on the diagonal of $R$, as
  before.
- **It is solved at $\kappa(A)^2$.** The part of $\tangent^*$ that comes from $b$ is $R^{-1} d$, one
  triangular solve at $\kappa(A)$. The part that comes from $\eta$ is $R^{-1} R^{-\top} \eta$, two,
  and is as sensitive as a solve of the normal equations: only the factor was computed at
  $\kappa(A)$. A linear term known to be $A\transpose c$ for some vector $c$ is better folded into
  $b$, as $b - c$, before factoring.

With $A$ empty, $\phi$ is linear and the problem is unbounded unless $\eta = 0$; with $b = 0$ and
$\eta$ a gradient, the minimizer is the solution of the normal equations with that gradient on the
right, which is the form of every method whose rows come without a residual.

## Eliminating one block

The variables usually come in *blocks*, $\tangent = (\tangent_1, \dots, \tangent_n)$, the
coordinates of one unknown each, and the rows in *factors*: a factor $f$ is a block of rows $A_f$
with a right-hand side $b_f$, over the few blocks $K_f$ whose columns of $A_f$ are not zero.
Linear terms are kept per block, $\eta_k$ on block $k$, since a linear term joins no two blocks.
The problem is

$$
	\phi(\tangent) = \sum_f \tfrac12 \bigl\lVert A_f\, \tangent_{K_f} - b_f \bigr\rVert^2 + \sum_k \eta_k\transpose \tangent_k , @tag(factored)
$$

and its matrix $A\transpose A$ has a nonzero block $(j, k)$ only when some factor has blocks for
both $j$ and $k$. *Eliminating* a block $k$ removes it from the problem. Collect the factors with a
block for $k$, call the other blocks they have the *separator* $S$ of $k$, and stack their rows
into $[A_k \;\, A_S \;\, b]$, the columns of $k$ first. Nothing else in [](eq:factored) involves
$\tangent_k$.

<Statement {...eliminateOne} />

<Proof>

Since $Q$ preserves lengths, the squares are

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\tfrac12 \lVert A_k \tangent_k + A_S \tangent_S - b \rVert^2
		&= \tfrac12 \left\lVert \begin{bmatrix} R \tangent_k + T \tangent_S - d \\ A' \tangent_S - b' \end{bmatrix} \right\rVert^2 \\
		&= \tfrac12 \lVert R \tangent_k + T \tangent_S - d \rVert^2 + \tfrac12 \lVert A' \tangent_S - b' \rVert^2 .
	\end{aligned}
$$

Write $w = R \tangent_k + T \tangent_S - d$. Since $R\transpose y = \eta_k$,

$$
	\eta_k\transpose \tangent_k = y\transpose R \tangent_k = y\transpose w - y\transpose T \tangent_S + y\transpose d ,
$$

and completing the square in $w$,

$$
	\tfrac12 \lVert w \rVert^2 + y\transpose w = \tfrac12 \lVert w + y \rVert^2 - \tfrac12 \lVert y \rVert^2 ,
$$

which together give the decomposition. Only its first term depends on $\tangent_k$; it is zero
exactly at the point of part 1, since $R$ is nonsingular, which proves part 1, and the substitution
$s = R \tangent_k$ turns its integral into that of a standard normal density's kernel, of volume
$(2\pi)^{d_k/2}$, times $\lvert \det R \rvert^{-1}$, which proves part 2. For part 3, $Q$ is
orthogonal, so $\Lambda$ is also the product of the reduced columns with themselves:
$\Lambda_{kk} = R\transpose R$, $\Lambda_{kS} = R\transpose T$ and
$\Lambda_{SS} = T\transpose T + A'^\top A'$, whence

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\Lambda_{SS} - \Lambda_{Sk} \Lambda_{kk}^{-1} \Lambda_{kS}
		&= T\transpose T + A'^\top A' - T\transpose R\, (R\transpose R)^{-1} R\transpose T \\
		&= A'^\top A' .
	\end{aligned}
$$

In the same way $g_k = \eta_k - R\transpose d$ and $g_S = \eta_S - T\transpose d - A'^\top b'$, so

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		g_S - \Lambda_{Sk} \Lambda_{kk}^{-1} g_k
		&= \eta_S - T\transpose d - A'^\top b' - T\transpose R^{-\top} (\eta_k - R\transpose d) \\
		&= \eta_S - A'^\top b' - T\transpose R^{-\top} \eta_k ,
	\end{aligned}
$$

which is the gradient of $\phi'$ at zero, since $R^{-\top} \eta_k = y$.

</Proof>

The first term of the decomposition is a *conditional*: it says what $\tangent_k$ is once
$\tangent_S$ is known. The second, $\phi'$, is a *new factor* on the separator, with the linear
term $-T\transpose y$ pushed onto the separator's blocks, and it replaces every factor that had a
block for $k$. The constant $\gamma$, and $\tfrac12 \lVert e \rVert^2$ when $A'$ is reduced
further, depend on nothing. The problem now has one block fewer and the same form. Minimizing over
$\tangent_k$ and integrating over it leave the same new factor, and differ only in a constant,
$\tfrac{d_k}{2} \log 2\pi - \log \lvert \det R \rvert$. Part 3 says that the new factor is what
Gaussian elimination of block $k$ from the normal equations would have produced, reached through
square roots instead: the matrix $\Lambda$ is never formed, and nothing is inverted but triangular
blocks.

The new factor has $m - d_k$ rows, which may be more than the separator's dimension $d_S$. A
further QR of $[A' \;\, b']$ leaves at most $d_S$ rows and a scalar residual, a constant, and is
worth doing when the factor is to be stored or eliminated again.

For example, take two scalars with a prior $\tangent_1 = 1$ and an odometry
$\tangent_2 - \tangent_1 = 1$, both of unit variance: the factors $[\,1 \;\, 0 \mid 1\,]$ and
$[\,{-1} \;\, 1 \mid 1\,]$, with no linear term. Eliminating $\tangent_1$ reduces their rows to

$$
	\begin{bmatrix} \sqrt2 & -1/\sqrt2 & 0 \\ 0 & 1/\sqrt2 & \sqrt2 \end{bmatrix} .
$$

The first row is the conditional $\tangent_1 = \tangent_2 / 2$, the second the new factor
$\tfrac12 (\tangent_2 / \sqrt2 - \sqrt2)^2$ on $\tangent_2$, minimized at $\tangent_2 = 2$, which
gives $\tangent_1 = 1$. Its matrix, $\tfrac12$, is the Schur complement $1 - 1 \cdot \tfrac12 \cdot
1$ of $\Lambda = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]$, so the
marginal variance of $\tangent_2$ is $2$: one unit from the prior, one from the odometry.

## Order, fill and the elimination tree

Which factors have a block for which variables is recorded by the *factor structure*, a bipartite
graph with a node for each block of variables and each factor, and an edge between a factor and
every block its rows have a nonzero block for. Eliminating $k$ removes $k$ and the factors joined to
it, and adds one factor joined to the whole separator. Blocks that shared no factor may now share
one, and the new nonzero blocks of the matrix this creates are its *fill*. The answer does not
depend on the order of elimination; the cost does, entirely.

<ModelGraph spec={starFactors} />

In the star, eliminating the hub $h$ first creates a factor over all the leaves at once, and with
$n$ leaves everything after it is dense work, of order $n^3$. Eliminating the leaves first creates,
for each, a new factor on $h$ alone, and the hub is eliminated last in a problem of one block: order
$n$. Finding the order with the least fill is NP-hard, but cheap heuristics do well. *Minimum
degree* eliminates, at each step, a block whose separator is then smallest, and its approximate
variants work on the factor structure without forming the matrix. An order may also be
*constrained* to eliminate chosen blocks last, which the reductions below need.

The separators depend on the factor structure and the order alone, not on any number, so the
elimination can be planned before it is performed. Give each block $k$ a *parent*: the block of its
separator that is eliminated first after $k$, which is the block its new factor will be collected
with next. The *elimination tree* has a node for each block and an edge from each block's parent to
the block. Its roots, which have no parent, are the blocks eliminated last, one in each connected
part of the factor structure; its leaves, which are no block's parent, are eliminated before
anything joined to them. The elimination of a block waits only on the blocks below it in the
elimination tree, so disjoint subtrees can be eliminated independently.

<ModelGraph spec={starTree} />

The plan, the order and the elimination tree with the separators, is the *symbolic* factorization,
and it is reused for as long as the factor structure is unchanged: by every iteration of an
optimizer, whose numbers change but not which factor touches which block, and by every problem of
one shape. Chains of blocks that each have one child and the child's separator less themselves can
be eliminated together, as one dense block, with no more fill; such groups are the cliques of a
*junction tree*, and eliminating each with one factorization does the same arithmetic in fewer,
larger dense operations.

## What the eliminated problem answers

Eliminating every block in turn, by part 1 of [%full](statement:eliminate-one), leaves only
conditionals and a constant. Stacked in the order of elimination, the conditionals' rows form one
block upper-triangular matrix $R$ with $R\transpose R = A\transpose A$ in that order, and right-hand
sides $d - y$; every computation with $R$ becomes a traversal of the elimination tree.

| computes | traversal | what it is for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the minimizer $\tangent^*$ | from the roots to the leaves: each block solves its conditional given its separator's values | the minimum of [](eq:factored) |
| $R^{-\top} c$ | from the leaves to the roots | a linear term's pushes $y$, and the first half of a normal-equations solve |
| $R^{-1} z$ | from the roots to the leaves | a draw from $\mathcal N(0, (A\transpose A)^{-1})$ when $z$ is standard normal |
| $\sum_i \log \lvert R_{ii} \rvert$ | none | $\tfrac12 \log \det A\transpose A$ |

The minimum is the sum of the constants, and the decrease from $\tangent = 0$ is
$\tfrac12 \lVert R \tangent^* \rVert^2$ by [](eq:decrease). Eliminating by integration instead, by
part 2, gives

$$
	\int_{\R^\dimension} e^{-\phi(\tangent)} \, \dd \tangent
	= (2\pi)^{\dimension / 2}\, e^{-\phi(\tangent^*)} \prod_i \lvert R_{ii} \rvert^{-1} . @tag(integral)
$$

**Partial elimination.** Eliminating only some blocks $E$ and keeping the rest, $K$, leaves the
conditionals of $E$ and the factors on $K$, old and new. The order is then constrained to put $K$
last, and only the factors joined to a block of $E$, directly or through new factors, are ever read.

<Statement {...marginal} />

<Proof>

By part 2 of [%full](statement:eliminate-one), applied once for each block of $E$, the integral of
$e^{-\phi}$ over $\tangent_E$ is a constant times $e^{-\phi_K}$, and the marginal density is that
integral normalized. $\phi_K$ is quadratic with the matrix of part 3, applied repeatedly, which is
the Schur complement of $\Lambda_{EE}$ in $\Lambda$, positive definite since $\Lambda$ is; a normal
density's kernel with precision $P$ and minimizer $\mu$ is $e^{-\frac12 (\tangent - \mu)\transpose
P (\tangent - \mu)}$ up to a constant.

</Proof>

The marginal's precision, covariance and mean follow from one more factorization of the factors on
$K$, which brings them to a single triangular root $W$ with a right-hand side: the precision is
$W\transpose W$, the covariance $W^{-1} W^{-\top}$, and $\sum_i \log \lvert W_{ii} \rvert$ is half
the log-determinant of the precision. A root that is to be read as a whitening must often be lower
triangular, with a positive diagonal, which QR does not give directly: factor the rows with the
order of $K$'s coordinates reversed, reverse both the rows and the columns of the upper-triangular
result, and change the sign of every row whose diagonal entry is negative. Reversal is a
permutation $P$, with $P\transpose P = I$, and a change of sign is a diagonal orthogonal matrix, so
the result $W$ has $W\transpose W$ equal to the original matrix.

**Eliminating from some factors only.** The reductions above eliminate from the whole of
[](eq:factored). A different question keeps the factors not joined to $E$ out of it: which single
factor on $K$ can replace those joined to $E$, without changing the minimum, or the integral, over
$E$? Split $\phi = \phi_{\ni E} + \phi_{\not\ni E}$ into the factors joined to $E$ and the rest.
Since the rest does not involve $\tangent_E$,

$$
	\begin{gathered}
		\min_{\tangent_E} \phi = \Bigl(\min_{\tangent_E} \phi_{\ni E}\Bigr) + \phi_{\not\ni E} , \\
		\int e^{-\phi} \, \dd \tangent_E = e^{-\phi_{\not\ni E}} \int e^{-\phi_{\ni E}} \, \dd \tangent_E ,
	\end{gathered} @tag(replace)
$$

so the replacement is the elimination of $E$ from $\phi_{\ni E}$ alone, with the linear terms of
$E$'s blocks and the separator's linear terms that came from those factors. Eliminating $E$ from
the whole of $\phi$ would instead fold $\phi_{\not\ni E}$ into the new factors, and a problem that
kept those factors as well would count them twice.

## Damping

A step computed from [](eq:problem) is the minimizer of a model, and a model is often trusted only
near $\tangent = 0$. *Damping* penalizes the step's length, adding
$\tfrac12 \lambda\, \tangent\transpose D \tangent$ for a $\lambda \ge 0$ and a positive diagonal
$D$, so that the minimizer solves $(A\transpose A + \lambda D)\, \tangent = A\transpose b - \eta$.
The penalty is a sum of squares of its own,

$$
	\tfrac12 \lambda\, \tangent\transpose D \tangent = \sum_k \tfrac12 \bigl\lVert \sqrt\lambda\, D_k^{1/2} \tangent_k - 0 \bigr\rVert^2 , @tag(damping)
$$

one factor on each block $k$ with $D_k$ its part of $D$, so damping appends rows and nothing else:
each new factor is joined to one block, creates no fill, and leaves the factor structure, and so
the symbolic factorization, unchanged. It also makes every block's rows of full rank when
$\lambda > 0$.
